Botany
A fairly good knowledge of the botanical sciences existed in the Hindu India not only in the post-Vedic period, but also in the Vedic. The Vedic आर्यन्स् were aware of what is now termed ‘photosynthesis.’ The Arthaśāstra of कौटिल्य (300 B. C.) refers to gulma- vṛkṣa-आयुर्वेद (science of bushy plants and trees). A lot of information regarding soils, seed selection, sowing, germination, grafting and cutting, rotation of crops, plant classification and so on, is found in some पुराणस् like the Agnipurāṇa (A.

).
An account of the different parts of a plant body like the roots and shoots, stem and branches, leaves, flowers and fruits are found in the Ṛgveda, Atharva-veda, Taittirīya Saṁhitā and Vājasaneyī Saṁhitā.
